            "sampleAnswer": "The benefits of regular exercise are so extensive and so well-documented that it's difficult to overstate them—exercise is probably the single most effective intervention available for improving both physical and mental health across the lifespan. The physical benefits are the most widely known—regular exercise reduces the risk of cardiovascular disease, type 2 diabetes, certain cancers, and obesity; it strengthens muscles and bones; it improves sleep quality; and it extends healthy life expectancy in ways that no medication can match. The mental health benefits are equally significant and perhaps less widely appreciated—exercise has been shown to be as effective as antidepressants for mild to moderate depression, to reduce anxiety, to improve cognitive function and memory, and to provide a sense of accomplishment and self-efficacy that contributes to overall wellbeing. Exercise also has important social dimensions—team sports, group fitness classes, and running clubs provide opportunities for social connection and community that are valuable in their own right. I think one of the most important things to understand about exercise is that the benefits are dose-dependent but not linear—even small amounts of regular physical activity provide significant health benefits, and the biggest gains come from moving from complete inactivity to modest activity rather than from moving from moderate to intense exercise. This means that the most important thing for most people is simply to start moving regularly, rather than to pursue an intensive exercise regime.",
