            "sampleAnswer": "Regular rest and holidays are not luxuries but genuine necessities for sustained performance, health, and wellbeing, and I think the evidence from psychology, neuroscience, and occupational health is unambiguous on this point. The human brain and body are not designed for continuous, uninterrupted effort—they require periods of rest and recovery to consolidate learning, repair cellular damage, regulate mood and emotion, and restore the cognitive resources that are depleted by sustained mental work. The research on overwork consistently shows that beyond a certain number of hours, additional work produces diminishing returns in terms of output and quality, and that chronic overwork leads to burnout, health problems, and ultimately a significant reduction in productivity. Holidays and extended breaks serve a different function from daily rest—they allow people to achieve a deeper level of recovery, to gain perspective on their work and lives, to pursue interests and relationships that are neglected during busy periods, and to return to work with renewed energy and motivation. I think one of the most important insights from the research on rest is that the quality of rest matters as much as the quantity—passive rest, like watching television, is less restorative than active rest, like exercise, time in nature, or social connection. The most effective approach to rest is probably one that combines adequate sleep, regular physical activity, meaningful social connection, and periodic extended breaks from work.",
